error: output port `o` is driven in a shape whose synthesized form shows one of its writes a cycle after the simulator does: in more than one clock phase (on both sides of a `clk.tick().await`), or in a single phase after the first by a write an input read reaches on one control path and not on another. The hardware holds a write to the clock edge once an input read precedes it in its phase (its own operand, a branch condition, a wait loop's exit test); the simulator runs it at its cycle's opening when that read has no `clk.tick().await` after it in the iteration, when the body has no register (the read is an immediate passthrough), or on a control path that skips the read — so such a write lands one observation late in the hardware (measured, phase E and 2026-10-30). The linear multi-tick path has no mux form at all for a port written in two phases. Accepted shapes: every write of `o` either preceded by no input read in its phase (a read in another phase is fine) or by an input read on every path to it with a `clk.tick().await` after it, in a body with a register — in any mix. Otherwise declare `o` as `RegOut<…>` (a registered output, immune because it commits at the clock edge), read the input before every write of `o` in its phase or before none of them, or drive it in exactly one phase that no input read reaches. If this module exists to DEMONSTRATE the divergence, opt out explicitly with `#[hardware(sequential, allow_pretick_alignment)]`.
